# Changing agent configuration

Three rules that only fire when someone edits agent configuration, which is why
they lived uselessly in a backlog file until 2026-09-01: a conditional reminder
in a TODO list is read on the day someone greps for it, never on the day it
matters.

## `autoMode`: never omit `"$defaults"`

If `autoMode` is ever configured, its `allow` and `soft_deny` lists **must**
include `"$defaults"`. Omitting it does not add permissions on top of the
built-in safety blocks — it silently **replaces** them, so the blocks disappear
with no error and no log line. Source: `~/p/wiki/brain/topics/claude-code-practice.md`.

## A model-id change is a dependency upgrade, not a string edit

Changing a model id — in a wrapper, a pinned config, the codex or agy offload —
changes behaviour in three ways that do not announce themselves:

- **Parameter support moves.** `temperature` and `top_p` are accepted by some
  models and rejected or ignored by others; a wrapper that always sends them can
  start erroring, or worse, start being ignored.
- **The tokenizer moves.** Token budgets calibrated on one model are wrong on
  the next; inflation of 1.0-1.35x was reported moving to Sonnet 5, so a prompt
  that fitted a window may no longer fit.
- **Cost and latency move**, which is the part everyone does check.

So pin deliberately, verify the wrapper's parameters against the new model, and
shadow the change before routing real traffic to it. Source:
`~/p/wiki/brain/topics/agent-failure-modes.md`.

## Budgets worth copying into any custom agent loop

Two defaults measured by Deep Agents, both cheap to implement and both about the
same failure — a context window filling with material nobody will read again:

- **Offload a tool result over ~20k tokens to a file**, and keep only the path
  plus a short preview in context.
- **Summarize at ~85% of the window**, not at the wall, so the summarization
  itself has room to run.

Source: `~/p/wiki/brain/topics/agent-frameworks.md`.
